Subject: lottie: trim paths "individually" in stacking order with three or more paths

LottieGroup::prepare() reverses the order of the paths of a group with trim paths, so that the
builder, walking the children backwards, appends them to the merged path in stacking order:
"trim individually" trims that path as one. The loop swapped neighbours while moving forward,
which carries the first path to the end: a rotation, the same as a reversal only for two paths.
With three paths the order was 1, 3, 2, and the trim showed the wrong path.

Each run of mergeable children is now reversed.

Origin: Lordicon.
Base: thorvg/thorvg main 72a66415 (1.2.0). License: MIT, like ThorVG.
---
--- a/src/loaders/lottie/tvgLottieModel.cpp
+++ b/src/loaders/lottie/tvgLottieModel.cpp
@@ -569,23 +569,22 @@
         }
     }
 
-    //Reverse the drawing order if this group has a trimpath.
+    //Reverse the drawing order if this group has a trimpath: each run of mergeable children
+    //(paths) is reversed, so that the paths reach the trimming in their stacking order.
     if (!trimpath) return;
 
-    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < children.count - 1; ) {
-        auto child2 = children[i + 1];
-        if (!child2->mergeable() || child2->type == LottieObject::Transform) {
-            i += 2;
+    auto reversible = [&](uint32_t i) {
+        return children[i]->mergeable() && children[i]->type != LottieObject::Transform;
+    };
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < children.count; ) {
+        if (!reversible(i)) {
+            ++i;
             continue;
         }
-        auto child = children[i];
-        if (!child->mergeable() || child->type == LottieObject::Transform) {
-            i++;
-            continue;
-        }
-        children[i] = child2;
-        children[i + 1] = child;
-        i++;
+        auto j = i;
+        while (j + 1 < children.count && reversible(j + 1)) ++j;
+        for (auto a = i, b = j; a < b; ++a, --b) std::swap(children[a], children[b]);
+        i = j + 1;
     }
 }
 
